  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 302 PM EST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\n- Dry weather continues into Saturday morning\n\nThe forecast has remained quite stable over the last few days with\nlittle movement in the details through Saturday. High pressure \nwill dominate, located near or just off to the east of the Great \nLakes. The gulf is cut off through the end of the week, so there\nis high confidence that the dry weather continues. BUFKIT\noverviews show little in the way of moisture of any kind other\nthan some occasional cirrus moving through. \n\n- Chances for rain remain in the forecast this weekend\n\nModels have come into better agreement that the rain chances this\nweekend are more Saturday night and Sunday focused. The rain\nshowers will be along and ahead of a cold front that will sweep\nthrough during this time frame. The upper shortwave trough is\nquite progressive, so the precipitation should be confined to\nthose two periods. \n\nA secondary cold front passes through Sunday night which will\nbring in air cold enough to possibly generate some light lake \neffect rain/snow showers on Monday. Moisture is quite shallow but\nthere is plenty of instability with delta T's increasing to near \n20C on Monday. \n\n- Mild temperatures through Saturday then cooling into next week \n\nTemperatures will be well above normal continuing into Saturday\nwith highs in the 60s. Normal highs are in the 40s this time of\nyear and we will be 20+ degrees above normal the next couple of\ndays. Temperatures will tumble into early next week with highs\ntrending colder than normal back into the 30s.",
